# sprint_sec_fs_native_part2.oot — (Part 2 of sprint_sec_fs_native.oot)
# Continuation of `openOODA/audit/sprint_sec_fs_native.oot`.

---

`fs_copy_file` (`fs.oo:137-148`): whole-file `fs_read_file` then
`fs_write_file`. No dest-parent create. No mode. No symlink preserve.
Read Err is collapsed to `"fs_copy_file: read failed"` (lossy).
Match after `is_err` still binds `""` on the dead Err arm.

`fs_copy_tree` (`fs.oo:150-169`):

    if fs_is_dir(fr, src) {
        fs_mkdir_p(fw, dst);          // never errs
        for name in fs_read_dir(fr, src) {
            fs_copy_tree(..., src+"/"+name, dst+"/"+name);
        }
        return Ok(void);
    }
    return fs_copy_file(...);

`sh_copy` is this function plus `sh_arg_ok` on both strings.

### Follows symlinks
`oo_fs_is_dir` (`chs_rt_fs.c:117-122`): `stat`, not `lstat`.
`oo_fs_read_dir`: `opendir`, follows a dir symlink.
`oo_read_file`: `fopen(path.data)` — follows a file symlink.
Reads are **not** under `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.

Plant `src/leak → /etc` (dir) or `src/shadow → /etc/shadow` (file).
`fs_copy_tree` / `sh_copy` copies host bytes into `dst` (writes still
need writedir). FsReadCap is any-path read. This is copy-out, not
`cp -P`.

`readdir` skips `.` and `..` (`chs_rt_fs.c:107`). Names do not
contain `/`. Traversal is the symlink, not `../` in a dirent.

### No cycle / no depth
`src/loop → src` (or `src/up → src/..`):

    copy_tree(src)           // is_dir
      copy_tree(src/loop)    // is_dir via stat follow
        copy_tree(src/loop/loop)
          ...

No visited-inode set. No depth cap. Same class as `fs_walk`
(`fs.oo:50-76`, already residual). Recursion is host stack, not a
heap worklist. Deep or cyclic trees: stack smash / unbounded
mkdir+write under dest.

`fs_walk` residual is now also a **write** residual: copy_tree
mkdir_p + write_file each hop.

### is_dir 1024 vs write PATH_MAX
`oo_fs_is_dir` `to_cpath(..., 1024)`.
`oo_fs_mkdir` / `oo_write_file` `to_cpath(..., PATH_MAX)`.
`to_cpath` truncates silently (sprint12 already named this).

A src path ≥1024 bytes can classify a **prefix** as dir/not-dir.
Copy then mkdir/write a longer (or different) string. Classification
and mutate are not the same object.

### Recursion + swallowed mkdir_p
If `dst` mkdir_p lied (file in the way / outside writedir / relative):

- src dir + dest not actually a dir → child writes `dst/name` fail
  or (if dest is a symlink dir outside) `path_under_writedir` on
  the child may deny — or, if dest realpath is still under writedir,
  write lands in an unexpected real directory.
- src dir + dest missing → `write_file` parent realpath fails → Err
  on the first file. Empty src dir: **Ok** after a failed mkdir_p.
  Silent no-op success.

### TOCTOU on the is_dir fork
    if fs_is_dir(src) { read_dir / recurse }
    else              { copy_file }

Swap src dir→file between `is_dir` and `opendir`: empty list, Ok.
Swap file→dir between `is_dir` and `fopen`: read fails, Err.
Swap file→symlink-to-secret between `is_dir` and `fopen`: copy-out.

Not a check-then-openat. Two independent path lookups.

### Memory / shape
Whole file in one `String`. No size cap. Large file is host-heap DoS
under Fr+Fw. Not sendfile. NULs: `oo_read_file` keeps `len`; write
uses `content.len`. Adjacent: `buf[n]=0` after `oo_str_alloc_payload(sz)`
when `n==sz` is one-past (chs_rt_fs.c:47-51). Not owned this hunt.

No native copy of fifo/socket/device. `fopen`/`stat` decide. A fifo
`fopen` blocks. Residual hang, not a cap hole.

`sh_copy` adds `sh_arg_ok`. Direct `fs_copy_tree` has **no** char
gate. Product API is the ungated recurse.

| Vector | Path-A want | Live |
|--------|-------------|------|
| symlink dir in src | copy link or refuse | **follow + recurse** |
| symlink file in src | copy link or refuse | **follow + read** |
| cycle link | refuse / cap | **unbounded recurse** |
| depth | cap | **none** |
| dest mkdir fail | Err | **Ok then later fail/no-op** |
| dest file-in-the-way | Err | **mkdir_p Ok** |
| is_dir vs mutate race | one lookup | **two lookups** |
| path clamp | deny long | **1024 vs PATH_MAX truncate** |
| relative dest | deny or cwd policy | mkdir deny + **Ok** facade |
| metadata / mode | document | **dropped** (0666 write leaf) |

Do not call this `cp -r`. It is follow-everything recurse + lie-Ok
mkdir + whole-file rewrite.

---
HUNT 3 — sh_clean still rm -rf; injection if gate bypassed
---

Live (`sh.oo:38-45`):

    pub fn sh_clean(p: &ProcessCap, dir: String) -> Result[Void, String]
        requires chars_len(dir) > 0
    {
        if !sh_arg_ok(dir) { return Err("sh: rejected unsafe arg"); }
        let r = process_exec(p, "rm -rf " + dir);
        ...
    }

`process_exec` → `sys_exec(p, "sh", "-c", shell_cmd)` (`process.oo:34-38`).
Child env is DE1.8 (`OODA_*`/`OO_*` + `PATH=/usr/bin:/bin`). That is
not a quote. `shell_cmd` is one string. `rm` sees whatever `sh` word-splits.

No FsWriteCap. No writedir. No `unlinkat`. No `remove_tree`.
`fs_remove_file` is `unlink` of one leaf under writedir only
(`chs_rt_fs.c:123-131`). It cannot replace `rm -rf`.

### Gate (what it actually is)
`sh_arg_ok` (`sh.oo:21-34`): whitelist

    A-Za-z0-9 / . - _ = : + space

Reject: `;|&$`(){}<> \n\r'"\\` and anything else (incl. `* ? [ ] ~`
and non-ASCII; `char_at` is a UTF-8 scalar, `char_is_alpha` is
`len==1 && isalpha`).

Empty string: loop does not run → **true**. `requires chars_len(dir)>0`
is the empty check, if emit lowers it (`c_emit_contract.oo`). Do not
treat `requires` as a second security gate on the interpreter path
until a prove says so.

Smoke (`scripts/sh_arg_ok_smoke.oo`) only refuses `;` `&&` `$(`.
It does **not** refuse space, `-`, `=`, `../`, `/`.

### In-class (gate not bypassed — still destuctive)
These **pass** `sh_arg_ok`:

| `dir` | `sh -c` line | Effect |
|-------|--------------|--------|
| `/` | `rm -rf /` | GNU rm refuses without `--no-preserve-root`; still a wipe attempt |
| `--no-preserve-root /` | `rm -rf --no-preserve-root /` | **GNU wipe** |
| `foo /` | `rm -rf foo /` | second operand is `/` |
| `foo /tmp` | `rm -rf foo /tmp` | two trees |
| `-rf /home` | `rm -rf -rf /home` | extra flags + path |
| `--one-file-system x` | flag injection | tool semantics |
| `../../` | `rm -rf ../../` | cwd-relative climb |
| `/tmp/wd/../outside` | climb | **not** writedir-bound |

Space is the multi-argv hole. `-` is the option hole. `/` and `..`
are the path hole. The class was built so `+x`, `https://`, and
`-d /tmp/foo -t x` keep compiling. It cannot be a single-path seal.

`sh_clean` is the worst sink left: the prefix is already `-rf`.

Live caller shape (not an inject prove; shows ProcessCap + path
concat still hits this function):

    scripts/ooda_emit_health.oo:56  sh_clean(p, err_base + "*.err")
    → `*` **rejected** now (FP vs old glob clean)

    scripts/fs_writedir_smoke.oo:70  sh_clean(p, wd)
    → if `wd` is an absolute `/tmp/...` string, **allowed**,
      and delete is **not** limited to OODA_FS_WRITEDIR

### If the gate is bypassed
Bypass = `sh_arg_ok` not called, or a string that should fail
reaches `process_exec`. Then the pre-DE1 matrix is live again:

| Payload | Line | Result |
|---------|------|--------|
| `x; id` | `rm -rf x; id` | **RCE** |
| `x && id` | `&&` | **RCE** (`&` not in class; only if bypass) |
| `x\|id` | pipe | **RCE** |
| `x$(id)` | subst | **RCE** |
| `x`id`` | backticks | **RCE** |
| `x $OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` | `$` | env expand in **sh**, not in child `printenv` |
| `x\nid` | newline | second command |
| `*` / `/*` | glob | `rm -rf /*` if shell globbing |

`oo_sys_exec` does not parse `shell_cmd`. Fork+execvp of
`sh -c <string>`. Parent IFS/PATH/LD_* are stripped. That does
not stop metacharacters **inside** the string.

Bypass routes (honesty, not a live probe):

1. Call `process_exec` / `sys_exec` directly with the same concat.
2. Future edit drops the `if !sh_arg_ok` and keeps the concat.
3. `sh_arg_ok` on a different string than the one concatenated
   (not today's code; one `dir`).
4. Interpreter / seed path that does not emit `requires` and
   somehow skips the `if` — not claimed; not disproved here.

The gate is a filter in front of a shell. It is not argv. It is
not a cap. Bypass restores Critical injection. **Non-bypass
already has wipe-class option injection.**

git / tar / chmod remain shell on the same gate (`sh.oo:62-99`).
Out of this hunt except as proof the class was never a tool seal.
See `audit/sprint_sec_sh.oot`.


*(Continued in `openOODA/audit/sprint_sec_fs_native_part3.oot`)*

